---
import Content from "@/layouts/Content.astro";
import { trailTo } from "../_shelves";
import AdSlot from "@/components/site/AdSlot.astro";
import { STROKE_PATTERNS } from "@/engine/sheets/writing/strokes";
import {
  PENMANSHIP_SHEETS,
  STOCKS,
  hrefFor,
  penmanshipShelf,
} from "../_penmanship";
import HubLinks from "../_HubLinks.astro";

/**
 * The penmanship subject hub — one of the shelves `docs/printables.md` §8
 * names.
 *
 * A listing rather than a sheet, like the other hubs. The one job it does
 * that the sheet pages can't is to say what the shelf is *for*: a parent whose
 * child can already form every letter searches for "neat handwriting" or
 * "handwriting speed", not for any sheet here by name, so the sections after
 * the listing make the case for the shelf before they point at a page.
 */
const title =
  "Free printable penmanship worksheets — strokes, sizes, spaces and speed | School Skills";
const description =
  "Printable penmanship practice: pencil strokes, letter families, tall and small letters, finger spaces, circle your best, a timed alphabet. Free, no account.";

const groups = penmanshipShelf();
const letter = STOCKS[0];
---

<Content
  title={title}
  description={description}
  world="paper"
  breadcrumb={trailTo("penmanship")}
  jsonLd={{
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "CollectionPage",
    name: "Printable penmanship worksheets",
    description,
    url: "https://schoolskills.app/printables/penmanship",
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    inLanguage: "en",
    hasPart: PENMANSHIP_SHEETS.map((sheet) => ({
      "@type": "LearningResource",
      name: sheet.heading,
      url: `https://schoolskills.app${hrefFor(sheet, letter)}`,
      learningResourceType: "Worksheet",
      teaches: sheet.teaches,
      typicalAgeRange: sheet.ages,
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
    })),
  }}
>
  <header class="hero wrap">
    <p class="hero__eyebrow">
      <span class="hero__world">The Print Shop</span>
      Penmanship
    </p>
    <h1 class="hero__title">Printable penmanship worksheets</h1>
    <p class="hero__lead">
      {PENMANSHIP_SHEETS.length} sheets for the child who can form every letter and
      still hands in a page nobody can read. The strokes letters are made of, the
      size of a letter against its neighbours, the space between words, judging your
      own work, and staying neat when the clock is running.
    </p>
    <p class="hero__note">Free &middot; two paper sizes &middot; no download</p>
  </header>

  {
    /*
      What exists, first — before the page explains itself. Somebody who came
      looking for finger spaces should be one link from finger spaces.
    */
  }
  <section class="band band--tight wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">On the shelf now</h2>
    <p class="h2__sub">
      Each of these is a page that is itself the worksheet &mdash; open it,
      press print, and that is the whole of it. Every sheet comes on US Letter
      and on A4, because a half-inch rule shrunk to fit whatever is in the tray
      is no longer a half-inch rule.
    </p>
    {
      groups.map((group) => (
        <>
          <h3 class="h2 h2--next">{group.label}</h3>
          <p class="h2__sub">{group.blurb}</p>
          <div class="prose">
            <ul>
              {group.sheets.map((sheet) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={hrefFor(sheet, letter)}>{sheet.name}</a> &mdash;{" "}
                  {sheet.summary}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </>
      ))
    }
  </section>

  <div class="wrap"><AdSlot name="article" /></div>

  <section class="band band--tight wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">Neat is not the goal &mdash; readable at speed is</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Handwriting that is neat but slow is the problem this shelf exists for.
        While forming a letter still takes attention, there is none left over
        for what is being written &mdash; the sentence is lost somewhere between
        the <em>b</em> and the <em>e</em> &mdash; and children who write fluently
        write more and write better, because the hand has stopped asking the mind
        for help. So the aim is not a neater page. It is a page that stays readable
        when the writing has to keep up with the thinking.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why the shelf ends with a clock. Writing the alphabet in order
        from memory against a timer is the task used to measure how fluent a
        hand is, and it says more about how well a child will write a paragraph
        than a neatness mark does. The sheets before it are what to do when the
        count comes back low.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--inset">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="h2">The four things that make a page readable</h2>
      <p class="h2__sub">
        The shape of each letter, its size against the letters beside it, the
        space between the words, and a slant that stays the same.
      </p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A letter the wrong shape is the handwriting shelf&rsquo;s business,
          and the letter families sheet here is where that shelf hands over: the
          same letters, grouped by the stroke they start with. The next two are
          this shelf&rsquo;s own &mdash; a sheet for the three heights a small
          letter stands at, and a sheet for the space a finger goes in &mdash;
          because a page of correctly formed letters can still be unreadable if
          the <em>a</em> is as tall as the <em>b</em> or the words run together.
        </p>
        <p>
          Slant is the one of the four not here. The models on these sheets are
          upright, as the printed ones are, and a sheet cannot teach a lean it
          does not show; a child taught a sloped hand has a scheme with its own
          paper for it. What every sheet on the shelf does teach is the thing
          underneath all four &mdash; a stroke that is the same size and the
          same shape each time, all the way to the end of the line.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--tight wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">Strokes before letters</h2>
    <p class="h2__sub">
      Nine patterns, in the order they are taught: straight before curved,
      lifted before continuous, and the two loops last.
    </p>
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        {
          STROKE_PATTERNS.map((pattern) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{pattern.label}</strong> &mdash; {pattern.blurb}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>
        A letter is two or three of these joined, and a hand that can draw one
        evenly, at speed, to the end of a line can form every letter that starts
        with it. The order matters: most children can copy a straight line at
        about two, a circle at three, and a slant only after four, which is why
        the pre-writing sheet runs in that order and why the slants are the hard
        row. The strokes are drawn as shapes rather than as letters, so the same
        row prints in any face &mdash; the sheet for a three-year-old and the
        cursive one for a ten-year-old are the same page with different rows on
        it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--tight wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">Handwriting first, penmanship after</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        These sheets assume the letters. A child still deciding which way round
        a <em>b</em> goes needs the handwriting shelf &mdash; trace it, copy it, write
        it &mdash; and a child learning to join needs the cursive one. Every sheet
        here is the page after those, for the day the letters are right and the page
        still isn&rsquo;t.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero__actions">
      <a class="cta" href="/printables/handwriting">Handwriting worksheets</a>
      <a class="cta cta--quiet" href="/printables/cursive">
        Cursive worksheets
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--tight wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">Your own words on the same sheet</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        This week&rsquo;s spellings to write five times and circle the best of,
        a sentence of your own for the finger spaces, a line to copy against the
        clock &mdash; the builder takes typed text and prints it as any of the
        sheets above, on any ruling, at any size. The settings travel in the
        link, so a sheet you make can be sent to another family as it stands.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        The <em>Name:</em> line is printed blank and filled in with a pencil. Nothing
        is uploaded to make a sheet and there is nowhere in a shared link to put a
        child&rsquo;s name &mdash; see <a href="/privacy"
          >what we don&rsquo;t collect</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero__actions">
      <a class="cta" href="/printables/make">Open the sheet builder</a>
      <a class="cta cta--quiet" href="/printables">Back to The Print Shop</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <HubLinks shelf="penmanship" />
</Content>
